1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,025
Hi everyone, welcome to our project.

2
00:00:02,025 --> 00:00:03,600
We looked at why flights get

3
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:05,231
delayed in the United States:

4
00:00:05,231 --> 00:00:06,750
where the delays come from,

5
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:08,662
how they build up through the day,

6
00:00:08,662 --> 00:00:11,250
and what a traveller can actually do about it.

7
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:13,342
We are Class K, Team One.

8
00:00:13,342 --> 00:00:15,771
The slide shows our six team members,

9
00:00:15,771 --> 00:00:17,412
and together we cover the

10
00:00:17,412 --> 00:00:19,711
different roles this project needs.

11
00:00:19,711 --> 00:00:22,140
We'll take you through what we found.

12
00:00:22,590 --> 00:00:25,077
Let me start with our main conclusion,

13
00:00:25,077 --> 00:00:27,826
because it shapes everything that follows.

14
00:00:27,826 --> 00:00:30,182
After studying ten years of flights,

15
00:00:30,182 --> 00:00:31,295
we found delay is

16
00:00:31,295 --> 00:00:33,193
predictable in its structure,

17
00:00:33,193 --> 00:00:35,680
even though individual flights aren't.

18
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:37,382
About three quarters comes

19
00:00:37,382 --> 00:00:38,952
from the airline itself,

20
00:00:38,952 --> 00:00:41,636
and a few flights carry most of the pain.

21
00:00:41,636 --> 00:00:44,516
I'll walk you through the problem, the data,

22
00:00:44,516 --> 00:00:46,610
and the five findings behind it.

23
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:49,850
Here's the problem that got us interested.

24
00:00:49,850 --> 00:00:51,711
Every year the United States

25
00:00:51,711 --> 00:00:53,571
publishes one average delay,

26
00:00:53,571 --> 00:00:55,033
and that single number

27
00:00:55,033 --> 00:00:56,893
hides two different stories.

28
00:00:56,893 --> 00:00:58,488
The typical flight lands

29
00:00:58,488 --> 00:01:00,082
about six minutes early,

30
00:01:00,082 --> 00:01:02,873
while the average says seven minutes late,

31
00:01:02,873 --> 00:01:04,666
because a handful of really

32
00:01:04,666 --> 00:01:06,194
bad flights drag it up.

33
00:01:06,194 --> 00:01:07,457
So the question is:

34
00:01:07,457 --> 00:01:09,450
which story should we believe?

35
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:11,928
That led us to four questions.

36
00:01:11,928 --> 00:01:14,768
Where do delay minutes actually come from,

37
00:01:14,768 --> 00:01:17,472
and is that split stable over ten years?

38
00:01:17,472 --> 00:01:19,230
How much delay passes from

39
00:01:19,230 --> 00:01:20,785
one flight to the next?

40
00:01:20,785 --> 00:01:22,272
Do airports group into

41
00:01:22,272 --> 00:01:23,962
their own delay patterns?

42
00:01:23,962 --> 00:01:25,923
And can we turn all this into

43
00:01:25,923 --> 00:01:27,951
a tool a traveller can act on?

44
00:01:27,951 --> 00:01:30,182
These four questions map directly

45
00:01:30,182 --> 00:01:31,940
onto the rest of the talk.

46
00:01:32,390 --> 00:01:33,571
To answer these,

47
00:01:33,571 --> 00:01:36,596
we brought together five public datasets:

48
00:01:36,596 --> 00:01:38,145
flight-level records,

49
00:01:38,145 --> 00:01:40,211
the delay-cause cross-check,

50
00:01:40,211 --> 00:01:43,532
hourly weather from N O A A, airport details,

51
00:01:43,532 --> 00:01:44,860
and airline codes.

52
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:46,409
Everything is public,

53
00:01:46,409 --> 00:01:49,287
so anyone can check our work. In total,

54
00:01:49,287 --> 00:01:52,017
that's over sixty-three million rows,

55
00:01:52,017 --> 00:01:54,083
and that scale is what makes

56
00:01:54,083 --> 00:01:55,780
our results believable.

57
00:01:56,230 --> 00:01:58,320
Our whole analysis runs as

58
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:00,651
one pipeline with six stages:

59
00:02:00,651 --> 00:02:04,107
acquire, attribute, analyse the heavy tail,

60
00:02:04,107 --> 00:02:07,483
trace the cascade, model, and communicate.

61
00:02:07,483 --> 00:02:09,332
Each stage is a script,

62
00:02:09,332 --> 00:02:12,547
so one command regenerates every figure,

63
00:02:12,547 --> 00:02:15,039
and fifty-three tests pin every

64
00:02:15,039 --> 00:02:17,129
number back to its source.

65
00:02:17,129 --> 00:02:18,977
We built it this way so

66
00:02:18,977 --> 00:02:20,826
nothing is a black box,

67
00:02:20,826 --> 00:02:23,800
and anyone can reproduce our results.

68
00:02:24,250 --> 00:02:25,892
Our first finding answers

69
00:02:25,892 --> 00:02:27,403
where delay comes from.

70
00:02:27,403 --> 00:02:29,571
Seventy-three point three percent

71
00:02:29,571 --> 00:02:31,805
traces back to the airline itself,

72
00:02:31,805 --> 00:02:33,841
and only twenty-six point seven

73
00:02:33,841 --> 00:02:35,943
percent to the system around it.

74
00:02:35,943 --> 00:02:38,571
What surprised us is how stable this is:

75
00:02:38,571 --> 00:02:41,198
it barely moved across the whole decade,

76
00:02:41,198 --> 00:02:43,104
which tells us where airlines

77
00:02:43,104 --> 00:02:45,140
can actually make a difference.

78
00:02:45,590 --> 00:02:48,615
Our second finding is about concentration.

79
00:02:48,615 --> 00:02:51,856
The Gini index is zero point eight seven six,

80
00:02:51,856 --> 00:02:54,089
meaning a tiny share of flights

81
00:02:54,089 --> 00:02:55,962
creates most of the delay.

82
00:02:55,962 --> 00:02:57,042
In plain terms,

83
00:02:57,042 --> 00:03:00,139
a few bad flights cause most of the misery.

84
00:03:00,139 --> 00:03:03,308
So we report percentiles and the heavy tail,

85
00:03:03,308 --> 00:03:05,325
and we re-run every headline

86
00:03:05,325 --> 00:03:07,630
with the pandemic years removed.

87
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:10,132
Our third finding is about how

88
00:03:10,132 --> 00:03:12,185
delay spreads through the day.

89
00:03:12,185 --> 00:03:15,058
Late aircraft is the biggest single cause,

90
00:03:15,058 --> 00:03:17,521
at thirty-eight point eight percent.

91
00:03:17,521 --> 00:03:19,436
About six percent of flights

92
00:03:19,436 --> 00:03:20,873
start already behind,

93
00:03:20,873 --> 00:03:22,583
and some of that delay is

94
00:03:22,583 --> 00:03:24,635
inherited from an earlier leg.

95
00:03:24,635 --> 00:03:26,893
So one delayed flight can quietly

96
00:03:26,893 --> 00:03:28,740
push the next one late too.

97
00:03:29,190 --> 00:03:30,741
Findings four and five

98
00:03:30,741 --> 00:03:32,434
are where we're careful.

99
00:03:32,434 --> 00:03:35,395
Airports do sort into five delay profiles,

100
00:03:35,395 --> 00:03:37,933
but that grouping isn't very stable,

101
00:03:37,933 --> 00:03:39,978
so we report it with caution.

102
00:03:39,978 --> 00:03:42,587
And our model can rank risky flights,

103
00:03:42,587 --> 00:03:45,619
but it can't promise an exact arrival time.

104
00:03:45,619 --> 00:03:47,876
Being honest about what we can't

105
00:03:47,876 --> 00:03:49,850
claim is part of the result.

106
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:52,365
All of this comes together in

107
00:03:52,365 --> 00:03:54,216
one interactive dashboard.

108
00:03:54,216 --> 00:03:57,278
Change the year, the airline, or the month,

109
00:03:57,278 --> 00:03:59,557
and every panel recomputes live.

110
00:03:59,557 --> 00:04:01,693
We also ran a usability check,

111
00:04:01,693 --> 00:04:03,331
with three timed tasks,

112
00:04:03,331 --> 00:04:06,321
using someone who isn't a data specialist.

113
00:04:06,321 --> 00:04:09,169
So the insight isn't locked in a report;

114
00:04:09,169 --> 00:04:12,160
it's something you can actually play with.

115
00:04:12,610 --> 00:04:14,210
On how we work together:

116
00:04:14,210 --> 00:04:16,944
we split the eight course roles among the

117
00:04:16,944 --> 00:04:19,745
six of us and rotate them halfway through.

118
00:04:19,745 --> 00:04:21,612
We decide things as a group,

119
00:04:21,612 --> 00:04:23,480
and every piece of work gets

120
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:25,080
checked by someone else.

121
00:04:25,530 --> 00:04:26,525
For our timeline,

122
00:04:26,525 --> 00:04:28,809
we planned backwards from the deadline.

123
00:04:28,809 --> 00:04:30,331
We've already finished the

124
00:04:30,331 --> 00:04:31,736
proposal and this video,

125
00:04:31,736 --> 00:04:34,078
we'll present in class later this month,

126
00:04:34,078 --> 00:04:35,308
and we'll wrap up the

127
00:04:35,308 --> 00:04:36,830
project by early December.

128
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:37,796
Finally,

129
00:04:37,796 --> 00:04:40,507
we set clear quality checks for ourselves,

130
00:04:40,507 --> 00:04:42,314
and we're upfront about what

131
00:04:42,314 --> 00:04:44,056
the model can and can't do:

132
00:04:44,056 --> 00:04:45,734
it's good at ranking risk,

133
00:04:45,734 --> 00:04:48,380
but it won't promise exact arrival times.

134
00:04:48,830 --> 00:04:50,232
Thank you for watching.

135
00:04:50,232 --> 00:04:51,390
That's our project.
